---
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { OPERATOR, LEGAL_VERSION, addressLines } from "../data/operator";
const address = addressLines("en").join(", ");
const toc: [string, string][] = [
  ["preamble", "Preamble"],
  ["subject", "Subject and duration"],
  ["data", "Data and data subjects"],
  ["instructions", "Instructions"],
  ["obligations", "Processor obligations"],
  ["subprocessors", "Sub-processors"],
  ["transfers", "Third countries"],
  ["assistance", "Assistance"],
  ["deletion", "Deletion and return"],
  ["audits", "Information and audits"],
  ["controller", "Controller obligations"],
  ["final", "Final provisions"],
  ["annex-1", "Annex 1: Security measures"],
  ["annex-2", "Annex 2: Sub-processors"],
];
---
<Legal title="Data Processing Agreement (DPA) | Connection Guard" h1="Data processing agreement" path="/dpa" lang="en"
  description="Data processing agreement under Art. 28 GDPR for server operators who link a Minecraft server to the Connection Guard dashboard."
  alternate={{ lang: "de", path: "/avv", label: "Deutsche Fassung (AVV)" }} toc={toc}>

  <p><strong>Version {LEGAL_VERSION}.</strong> This is a translation; the German <a href="/avv" hreflang="de">Auftragsverarbeitungsvertrag</a> is legally binding. This agreement under Art. 28(3) GDPR applies between the operator of the Minecraft servers linked to the dashboard (<strong>controller</strong>) and {address} (<strong>processor</strong>). It is concluded electronically (Art. 28(9) GDPR). The controller or a person authorised to enter into the agreement on its behalf accepts it for the network when linking or in the dashboard. Acceptance applies to all servers linked to that network. The dashboard records the network, account, version and time.</p>

  <h2 id="preamble">1. Preamble</h2>
  <p>The Connection Guard plugin checks players' connections on the controller's server. When the controller links a server to the dashboard at app.connectionguard.net, the plugin sends the results of these checks to the processor so that the controller can view them and manage the server there. The processor processes these results solely on the controller's behalf. Separately transmitted network signals for Connection Guard Intel are outside this agreement; see the <a href="/privacy#network-signals">privacy policy</a> for how they are processed.</p>

  <h2 id="subject">2. Subject matter, nature, purpose and duration</h2>
  <p><strong>Subject matter and purpose:</strong> providing the dashboard for the controller's linked servers. This covers receiving, storing, analysing, displaying and deleting check results, and sending the settings and commands the controller triggers in the dashboard to the controller's servers.</p>
  <p><strong>Nature of processing:</strong> receipt, storage, organisation, retrieval, search, aggregation into statistics, display, transmission to the controller's server and erasure. Names reported at login are used for display and search in the decision log. For entries without a reported name, public profile names for verified UUIDs may be looked up for display (section 6).</p>
  <p><strong>Duration:</strong> while the relevant server is linked. Turning the cloud connection off in the plugin stops transmission but does not delete data already stored. Processing for a server ends when it is unlinked or its network is deleted; section 9 applies to return and deletion.</p>

  <h2 id="data">3. Types of personal data and categories of data subjects</h2>
  <p><strong>Data subjects:</strong> players who connect to a linked server of the controller or who are named in its rules and exemptions.</p>
  <p><strong>Types of data:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Per login check: time, IP address, verified UUID where available, identity trust status, verdict and reason, rules applied, and answers from the detection services (country, network or ASN, provider, risk score, VPN or proxy detection). From plugin 0.6.2 a check can also contain the name the player reported at login. The name alone is not proof of identity. The dashboard marks unverified identities.</li>
    <li>Entries in exemptions and access rules set by the controller: player name, UUID or IP address and an optional note.</li>
    <li>For entries without a reported name: public Minecraft profile names for their UUIDs, where available. These are cached for up to 24 hours and are not added to the decision log.</li>
    <li>Hourly statistics derived from these, which no longer identify individuals.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Special categories of personal data (Art. 9 GDPR) are outside the scope of this agreement. The controller must not enter such data in dashboard free-text fields.</p>

  <h2 id="instructions">4. Instructions</h2>
  <p>The processor processes the data only on documented instructions from the controller, including transfers to third countries (Art. 28(3)(a) GDPR). If Union or Member State law requires processing, the processor informs the controller of that requirement beforehand unless that law prohibits notification on important grounds of public interest. Instructions are this agreement, the plugin's configuration, and the controller's settings and actions in the dashboard (for example unlinking, settings, access rules). Further instructions are given in text form to {OPERATOR.privacyEmail}. If the processor considers an instruction unlawful, it informs the controller without delay.</p>

  <h2 id="obligations">5. Obligations of the processor</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It processes the data only for the purposes of this agreement and does not use it for its own purposes, except for statistics without personal reference that are needed to run the service, such as the number of active servers. No player data goes into these.</li>
    <li>Persons with access to the data are committed to confidentiality or are under a statutory obligation of confidentiality (Art. 28(3)(b) GDPR).</li>
    <li>It takes the technical and organisational measures under Art. 32 GDPR set out in Annex 1 and keeps them up to date without lowering the level of protection.</li>
    <li>It notifies the controller of personal data breaches without undue delay after becoming aware of them (Art. 33(2) GDPR), with the information the controller needs for its own notification. Notification is given by a notice in the dashboard and, where possible, through other channels.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="subprocessors">6. Sub-processors</h2>
  <p>Under this agreement the controller gives general written authorisation to engage further processors (Art. 28(2) GDPR). Annex 2 lists the sub-processors used. The processor imposes the same data protection obligations on them by contract and is liable to the controller for their performance (Art. 28(4) GDPR). It gives notice on this page and in the dashboard at least 30 days before adding or replacing a sub-processor, so that the controller can object. If the controller objects, both parties will consider a solution; the controller may end the link free of charge.</p>
  <p>To find missing public profile names, the dashboard looks up verified Minecraft UUIDs through Mojang/Microsoft profile services. The lookup does not send names reported by the plugin or search terms from the decision log. These profile services act as separate controllers for the lookup. Discord alerts sent by the dashboard contain no player data.</p>

  <h2 id="transfers">7. Processing outside the EU</h2>
  <p>The decisions database (Cloudflare D1) is restricted to the EU. Cloudflare operates the service on its global network, which may involve processing or access outside the European Economic Area. Such transfers take place in accordance with section 4 and only if the requirements of Art. 44 et seq. GDPR are met, for example under an applicable adequacy decision or the EU standard contractual clauses under Art. 46(2)(c) GDPR with any required supplementary measures.</p>

  <h2 id="assistance">8. Assistance to the controller</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Data subject rights:</strong> the dashboard supports search by IP address, UUID or stored name. The processor assists the controller on request with deletion or correction of individual records and other requests under Art. 12 to 23 GDPR. It promptly forwards requests received directly from players to the responsible controller where it can identify them, and responds only on their instruction.</li>
    <li><strong>Art. 32 to 36 GDPR:</strong> it assists the controller with security, breach notifications, data protection impact assessments and prior consultation, as far as it has the relevant information.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="deletion">9. Deletion and return</h2>
  <p>Login check entries are deleted after 30 days in a daily cleanup; hourly statistics are deleted after about 13 months. Unlinking a server removes its entries from the active database. If the controller wants the data returned, it can instruct the processor by emailing {OPERATOR.privacyEmail} before deleting it.</p>
  <p>When the processing services end, the processor, at the controller's choice, deletes all remaining personal data processed on its behalf or returns it in a commonly used electronic format and deletes existing copies. This does not apply to data whose storage is required by Union or Member State law (Art. 28(3)(g) GDPR). Deleted records may remain in Cloudflare D1's automatic recovery history for up to seven days. That history is used only for recovery. On restore, any deletions instructed in the meantime are applied before the data is put back into use.</p>

  <h2 id="audits">10. Information and audits</h2>
  <p>The processor makes available all information necessary to demonstrate compliance with Art. 28 GDPR. It allows for and contributes to audits, including inspections, conducted by the controller or an auditor appointed by the controller (Art. 28(3)(h) GDPR). The source code of the dashboard and plugin is public. Audits are coordinated in text form to protect operations and other controllers' data.</p>

  <h2 id="controller">11. Obligations of the controller</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It ensures a legal basis for processing its players' data and informs players under Art. 13 and 14 GDPR, for example in its server's privacy notice.</li>
    <li>It links only servers it operates or is authorised for, and ensures that only authorised representatives accept this agreement for its network.</li>
    <li>It informs the processor without delay if it notices errors or irregularities in the processing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="final">12. Final provisions</h2>
  <p>Liability is governed by Art. 82 GDPR. Otherwise the <a href="/terms">terms of service</a> apply; where they conflict on data protection, this agreement prevails. A new version applies to an existing network only after the controller accepts it in the dashboard. The previously accepted version applies until then. German law applies. If a provision is invalid, the rest of the agreement remains valid.</p>

  <h2 id="annex-1">Annex 1: Technical and organisational measures (Art. 32 GDPR)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Physical security:</strong> no own servers. Operation only in data centres of the sub-processor Cloudflare, certified under ISO 27001 and audited under SOC 2.</li>
    <li><strong>Access control:</strong> dashboard sign-in only via Discord. Roles per network (owner, admin, viewer). Sessions expire after 30 days and are stored server-side only as a hash. Servers authenticate with a secret stored only as a hash. Only the processor has administrative access to the infrastructure.</li>
    <li><strong>Transmission:</strong> encrypted transport only (TLS). API keys and webhook addresses are encrypted with AES-GCM and deleted once the server confirms them.</li>
    <li><strong>Storage:</strong> database with EU jurisdiction, encrypted at rest. Check results are stored per network and shown only to its members.</li>
    <li><strong>Data minimisation:</strong> the plugin sends individual entries only after linking. From plugin 0.6.2, transmitted check results can include the reported name before the network has accepted this version of the agreement. The cloud checks acceptance on receipt and, without the applicable version, discards the name before storage and keeps the rest of the check result. Product analytics excludes areas displaying player data.</li>
    <li><strong>Input control:</strong> an audit log records who changed which settings, rules or links and when.</li>
    <li><strong>Availability:</strong> Cloudflare's distributed infrastructure. The plugin checks players independently of the dashboard, so an outage does not affect logins.</li>
    <li><strong>Separation and deletion:</strong> access is limited to the respective network. Automatic deletion after the periods in section 9.</li>
    <li><strong>Review:</strong> public source code, automated interface tests, and review of the measures whenever the service changes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="annex-2">Annex 2: Sub-processors</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Company</th><th scope="col">Service</th><th scope="col">Location</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Cloudflare, Inc., 101 Townsend Street, San Francisco, CA 94107, USA</td><td>Hosting, database, delivery, attack protection</td><td>Database in the EU (EU jurisdiction); processing in Cloudflare's global network, safeguarded under section 7</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Product analytics (PostHog) receives no player data and is therefore not a sub-processor under this agreement.</p>
</Legal>
